{{- define "task-decomposition" -}}

TASK DECOMPOSITION PRINCIPLE:
Default to one coding task per coherent outcome or root cause. Include its production
changes, required tests (unit, integration, and E2E), fixtures, and relevant docs in
the same task when they share one candidate and owner. These are deliverables of one
intent, not separate intents merely because they touch different files or packages.
Split only for independently useful outcomes, different ownership or permissions,
a risk/isolation boundary, or a real dependency that makes separate candidates safer.
For each split, explain why it is safer or faster than one task, accounting for actual
writer capacity and review/merge overhead. File count, package count, conjunctions,
and assertion count are signals to examine, not automatic split thresholds. Do not
combine unrelated outcomes or root causes merely to reduce the task count.
Declare dependency order between tasks — no circular or missing dependencies.
Every dependency MUST include dependency_contracts metadata naming exactly one provider
(provider_output for a sibling or provider_task for an existing task), its exact purpose,
the artifact/contract/acceptance check it supplies, lifecycle gate, and severity.
Gates are before_start, before_approval_merge, or advisory. before_start contracts must
exactly match depends_on/task_depends_on; later/advisory contracts must not be scheduler
dependencies. Severity consequences are deterministic: critical means the consumer
contract cannot be satisfied safely; high means stop at the declared gate; medium means
work may proceed only up to the declared gate; low is advisory-only. Put every final
acceptance check in the validation array of its one owning output task; when another task
supplies that check, name the provider and exact check in dependency_contracts. Validate
the complete cross-plan graph before approval; unresolved providers, missing generated
tasks, reverse validation cycles, and gates stronger than their declaration fail closed.
Shared-file rule: when two tasks modify the same file, they MUST have a depends_on
relationship. Parallel coders work in isolated worktrees — shared files without
dependency ordering cause merge conflicts. Audit scope overlaps before finalizing.
Parallelism objective: tasks without depends_on run concurrently — each dependency
link added serializes execution. Prefer wide task graphs over deep chains when
integration risk is manageable. When splitting, ask: "can these run in parallel
without touching the same files?" If yes, omit depends_on — wall-clock time shrinks.
If shared files force ordering, that's correct; don't remove real dependencies for speed.
Do not split cohesive work solely to expose parallelism when writer capacity is absent.
Verify the plan covers ALL spec requirements — gaps will be rejected.
TDD colocation: a behavior change and its unit tests are one intent, not two.
Never split "implement X" from "test X" — coders do TDD inside each task.
Integration tests spanning multiple independently owned outcomes MAY be separate tasks.

Cross-cutting deliverables: features that add or change user-visible behavior need
E2E coverage through the project's integration test layer and updates to affected
user-facing documentation and specifications. These are first-class deliverables,
not automatic separate tasks. Split them only under the criteria above; preserve
the independent code review and final integration/QA gates in either case.
If the feature is purely internal with no user-visible change, note "N/A" with justification in the spec compliance matrix.

REFACTORING TASKS:
When a task is pure structural refactoring (file split/move with no behavior change),
done_when MUST distinguish behavioral test changes (not expected) from mechanical test
updates (expected). Mechanical updates (file-path strings, import paths) that preserve
the test's invariant are expected and in-scope — include affected test files in scope.
